time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** Canary::Stability COMPATIBILITY AND SUPPORT CHECK"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** ================================================="
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** Hi!"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** I do my best to provide predictable and reliable software."
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** However, in recent releases, P5P (who maintain perl) have been"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** introducing regressions that are sometimes subtle and at other times"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** catastrophic, often for personal preferences with little or no concern"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** for existing code, most notably CPAN."
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** For this reason, it has become very hard for me to maintain the level"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** of reliability and support I have committed myself to in the past, at"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** least with some perl versions: I simply can't keep up working around new"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** bugs or gratituous incompatibilities, and in turn you might suffer from"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** unanticipated problems."
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** Therefore I have introduced a support and compatibility check, the results"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** of which follow below, together with a FAQ and some recommendations."
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** This check is just to let you know that there might be a risk, so you can"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** make judgement calls on how to proceed - it will not keep the module from"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** installing or working."
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** The stability canary says: (nothing, it was driven away by harsh weather)"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** It seems you are running perl version 5.038002, likely the \"official\" or"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** \"standard\" version. While there is nothing wrong with doing that,"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** standard perl versions 5.022 and up are not supported by CBOR::XS."
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** While this might be fatal, it might also be all right - if you run into"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** problems, you might want to downgrade your perl or switch to the"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** stability branch."
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** If everything works fine, you can ignore this message."
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** Stability canary mini-FAQ:"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** Do I need to do anything?"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***    With luck, no. While some distributions are known to fail"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***    already, most should probably work. This message is here"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***    to alert you that your perl is not supported by CBOR::XS,"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***    and if things go wrong, you either need to downgrade, or"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***    sidegrade to the stability variant of your perl version,"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***    or simply live with the consequences."
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** What is this canary thing?"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***    It's purpose is to check support status of CBOR::XS with"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***    respect to your perl version."
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** What is this \"stability branch\"?"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***    It's a branch or fork of the official perl, by schmorp, to"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***    improve stability and compatibility with existing modules."
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** How can I skip this prompt on automated installs?"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***    Set PERL_CANARY_STABILITY_NOPROMPT=1 in your environment."
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***    More info is in the Canary::Stability manpage."
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** Long version of this FAQ: http://stableperl.schmorp.de/faq.html"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="*** Stability Branch homepage: http://stableperl.schmorp.de/"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="***"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="Continue anyways?  [y] y"
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="Warning: prerequisite Task::Weaken 1.06 not found."
time="2025-03-18T20:36:27Z" level=debug msg="Checking if your kit is complete..."
